regulator: axp20x: force poly-phase setup for AXP323 DCDC1+DCDC2

On the AXP323 the DCDC1 and DCDC2 converters can be ganged into a
two-phase supply for one high-current rail.  Upstream only ever reads
the poly-phase bit in AXP323_DCDC_MODE_CTRL2 (in
axp20x_is_polyphase_slave(), to decide whether DCDC2 should be skipped
during registration); nothing in the kernel ever writes it.  Whether the
phases are ganged is therefore inherited from the boot firmware.

That breaks boards wired for poly-phase operation.  On the Radxa Cubie
A5E the mainline DT explicitly notes "DCDC2 is polyphased with DCDC1"
and uses the combined rail as vdd-cpub, supplying the four "big"
Cortex-A55 cores.  With the bit clear, DCDC1 alone must source the whole
cluster.  Voltage scaling still appears to succeed, but the rail cannot
deliver the current the cluster needs at higher operating points and the
SoC hangs hard, with no oops and nothing on the console.

Measured on a Cubie A5E (A527, 1 GB) with the big cluster driven through
its OPP table one step at a time under the userspace governor:

  408 MHz / 900 mV   ok
  672 MHz / 900 mV   ok
  840 MHz / 900 mV   ok
  1008 MHz / 900 mV  ok
  1200 MHz / 920 mV  ok
  1344 MHz / 960 mV  hard hang, watchdog reset

A busy-loop timing taken at each step scaled inversely with frequency,
confirming the cores really were running at the requested rate, so the
clock side was working correctly.  regulator_summary on the failing
kernel listed dcdc2 as an independent regulator with no consumers -
i.e. the poly-phase bit was clear - whereas on a boot where the same
board reached 1800 MHz it did not appear at all, as expected for a
poly-phase slave.

Set the bit explicitly during probe, before the registration loop that
consults axp20x_is_polyphase_slave(), so behaviour no longer depends on
what the bootloader left behind.

--- a/drivers/regulator/axp20x-regulator.c
+++ b/drivers/regulator/axp20x-regulator.c
@@ -1622,6 +1622,43 @@
 	/* This only sets the dcdc freq. Ignore any errors */
 	axp20x_regulator_parse_dt(pdev);
 
+	/*
+	 * On the AXP323, DCDC1 and DCDC2 can be ganged together as a
+	 * two-phase supply for a single high-current rail.  Upstream only
+	 * ever *reads* the poly-phase bit (see axp20x_is_polyphase_slave()
+	 * above); nothing in the kernel ever sets it, so whether the two
+	 * phases are ganged is inherited from whatever the boot firmware
+	 * happened to leave in AXP323_DCDC_MODE_CTRL2.
+	 *
+	 * That is a problem on boards that are physically wired for
+	 * poly-phase operation, such as the Radxa Cubie A5E, whose DT
+	 * explicitly documents "DCDC2 is polyphased with DCDC1" and uses
+	 * the combined rail as vdd-cpub (the four Cortex-A55 "big" cores).
+	 * If the bit is clear, DCDC1 alone has to source the whole cluster.
+	 * Voltage scaling still appears to work, but the rail browns out
+	 * once the cluster draws more current than a single phase can
+	 * deliver, which hangs the SoC hard.  Measured on a Cubie A5E: the
+	 * big cluster is stable up to 1200 MHz/920 mV and dies instantly at
+	 * 1344 MHz/960 mV, with no oops and no console output.
+	 *
+	 * Force the two phases together so the behaviour no longer depends
+	 * on the bootloader.  This has to happen before the registration
+	 * loop below, because axp20x_is_polyphase_slave() reads the bit to
+	 * decide whether to skip registering DCDC2 as an independent
+	 * regulator.
+	 */
+	if (axp20x->variant == AXP323_ID) {
+		ret = regmap_update_bits(axp20x->regmap,
+					 AXP323_DCDC_MODE_CTRL2,
+					 BIT(1), BIT(1));
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_err(&pdev->dev,
+				"failed to set AXP323 poly-phase mode: %d\n",
+				ret);
+			return ret;
+		}
+	}
+
 	for (i = 0; i < nregulators; i++) {
 		const struct regulator_desc *desc = &regulators[i];
 		struct regulator_desc *new_desc;
